In Goldstein's discharge tube, a perforated cathode let positive particles through to form ANODE RAYS. These rays:
 - travelled from anode to cathode (suggesting positive charge — opposite direction to electrons);
 - had a charge-to-mass ratio that varied with the gas in the tube;
 - had the SMALLEST mass when the gas was HYDROGEN — the same particle Rutherford later identified as the PROTON.

Since protons appeared from any gas in the tube (and were lightest with hydrogen), they must be a constituent of every atom — confirming the existence of positive subatomic particles.
